---
// English-only legal document. Restored from the pre-Astro site (4 June 2026),
// with section 2 rewritten: the original said the site ran no analytics and set
// no cookies, which stopped being true on 6 June 2026 when GA4 was added.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
import LegalFooter from '../components/LegalFooter.astro';
import { DEFAULT_LOCALE } from '../i18n/config';
import { REPO, COFFEE, CONTACT } from '../links';

const title = 'Privacy Policy — Home Rec';
const description =
  'Privacy Policy for Home Rec — a macOS app published under The Building Blocks Co.';
---
<Base
  locale={DEFAULT_LOCALE}
  path=""
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/privacy"
  alternates={false}
  appJsonLd={false}
>
  <SiteNav locale={DEFAULT_LOCALE} />

  <main class="legal">
    <div class="legal-inner">
      <a href="/" class="legal-back">← Back to Home Rec</a>

      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="legal-meta">Last updated: 26 July 2026</p>

      <div class="legal-note">
        <p>
          <strong>Change notice (26 July 2026).</strong> This site now uses Google Analytics,
          which sets cookies in your browser. An earlier version of this policy said the site
          ran no analytics and set no cookies. Section 2 below has been rewritten to describe
          what is actually collected.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="legal-note">
        <p>
          <strong>The short version:</strong> the Home Rec app runs entirely on your Mac. It
          makes no network calls, sends no telemetry, and has no analytics. Your recordings
          stay on your device and we never see them. This website is separate: it uses Google
          Analytics to count visits and Download clicks, which does set cookies. We mention
          every third party we rely on below — there are only a few.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>1. The Home Rec app</h2>
      <p>
        Home Rec captures audio playing through your Mac's system output and writes it to a
        local WAV file on your computer. The app uses Apple's <strong>ScreenCaptureKit</strong>
        framework, gated by the macOS Screen Recording permission you grant in
        <em class="ui">System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</em>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The app makes <strong>no network connections</strong> of any kind.</li>
        <li>It sends <strong>no telemetry, analytics, crash reports, or usage data</strong> to us or anyone else.</li>
        <li>Recordings are written only to the local folder you choose. We never see them.</li>
        <li>The app does not include any third-party SDKs that collect data.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can verify all of the above by reading the source code at
        <a href={REPO} target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/melissa-pereira-deel/home-rec</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>2. This website</h2>
      <p>
        This website is served as static HTML by our hosting provider, Vercel. Standard server
        logs (your IP address, user agent string, request time and path) may be kept by the host
        for operational purposes — see
        <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vercel's privacy policy</a>
        for retention details.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Google Analytics.</strong> This site loads Google Analytics 4 (measurement ID
        <em class="ui">G-56GPLH521X</em>) on every page, so we can see how many people visit and
        how many go on to download the app. It runs as soon as a page loads. Through it, Google
        processes on our behalf:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>which pages you view, when, and which site or search referred you;</li>
        <li>an <strong>approximate</strong> location (typically country, region and city) derived from your IP address — Google uses the IP address for this and does not log or store it in Analytics;</li>
        <li>technical details of your device: browser, operating system, screen size, and language;</li>
        <li>two interaction events we defined ourselves — <em class="ui">download_click</em> when a Download button is used, and <em class="ui">buy_me_a_coffee_click</em> when the support link is used. Each records only the link's URL and its visible label.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Cookies.</strong> Google Analytics sets first-party cookies in your browser —
        <em class="ui">_ga</em> and <em class="ui">_ga_&lt;id&gt;</em> — which hold a randomly
        generated identifier used to tell a returning visitor from a new one. They are not used
        to identify you by name, and we do not combine them with any other data.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>How long it is kept.</strong> Our Analytics property is set to retain event-level
        and user-level data for <strong>14 months</strong>, after which Google deletes it. Because
        we also have Google's "reset on new activity" option enabled, that 14 months is counted
        from your most recent visit rather than your first — so if you keep coming back, the window
        keeps moving. One caveat we would rather state plainly: this window covers the detailed
        underlying data, not the aggregate totals Google derives from it. Summary statistics, such
        as how many visits a month saw, are not deleted on that schedule.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>How to opt out.</strong> You can block this entirely: use a content blocker, turn
        off JavaScript for this site, or install Google's
        <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Analytics opt-out browser add-on</a>.
        Nothing on this site stops working if you do — the site is plain static HTML. Google's own
        terms for this data are in the
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Privacy Policy</a>
        and
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How Google uses data when you use our partners' sites</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not run A/B testing, advertising pixels, session recording, or heat mapping on this
        site, and we do not sell or share this data with anyone.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Donations (Buy Me a Coffee)</h2>
      <p>
        If you support the project at
        <a href={COFFEE} target="_blank" rel="noopener">buymeacoffee.com/melissadebritto</a>,
        your payment is processed entirely by Buy Me a Coffee under their own privacy policy:
        <a href="https://www.buymeacoffee.com/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">buymeacoffee.com/privacy-policy</a>.
        We only see what they share with us — typically your display name (if you provided one)
        and any message you chose to attach. We do not receive payment-card or banking information.
      </p>

      <h2>4. GitHub</h2>
      <p>
        Issues, pull requests, discussions, and other interactions on our repository at
        <a href={REPO} target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/melissa-pereira-deel/home-rec</a>
        are governed by
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub's Privacy Statement</a>
        and are public by default. We see whatever GitHub shows project maintainers (username,
        profile information you make public, contents of your contributions).
      </p>

      <h2>5. Your rights (LGPD / GDPR)</h2>
      <p>
        The app collects nothing, so there is nothing held about you there. For this website, the
        analytics data described in section 2 is pseudonymous — it is tied to a random cookie
        identifier, not to your name or email — which means we usually cannot connect it to you
        even if you ask us to. The most reliable way to remove it is to clear this site's cookies
        in your browser and opt out using one of the methods above.
      </p>
      <p>
        For data held by our processors (Google, Buy Me a Coffee, GitHub, Vercel), please also
        contact them directly using the links above — they are best positioned to act on requests
        concerning data they hold about you.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you believe we hold information about you, or you have any other privacy question,
        email us at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. We aim to respond within 30 days.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        Home Rec is an open-source project by <strong>Melissa de Britto</strong>, an individual
        developer, published under The Building Blocks Co. brand. "The Building Blocks Co." is the
        name used for projects published by Melissa de Britto; it is not a separate legal entity.
      </p>
      <p>
        Data controller for the purposes of LGPD (Brazil) and GDPR (EU):
        <strong>Melissa de Britto</strong>. Contact:
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        We may update this policy from time to time. The current version is always at this URL,
        with the "Last updated" date above. Material changes will be highlighted at the top of
        this page for at least 30 days.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>

  <LegalFooter sibling="terms" />
</Base>
